        "text": "There is something about rain that makes the world honest. It strips away the easy brightness, the cheerful advertisements of color, and leaves behind a quieter palette. Everything becomes slightly softer, slightly more forgiving, because wet surfaces do not lie. They reflect what is actually there: streetlamps, tired faces, the underside of leaves.\n\nI think people who love rain are not necessarily melancholic. They have simply learned to appreciate the version of reality that appears when the sun steps back. Rain slows footsteps. It turns umbrellas into small, private shelters. It gives strangers a shared vocabulary of inconvenience, and sometimes that is enough to make us feel like neighbors.\n\nI remember once standing under a shop awning during a sudden downpour, surrounded by people I would never have spoken to otherwise. A woman offered me a mint. A man pointed at the sky and said, \u201cIt\u2019s really coming down now,\u201d as if the sky needed a narrator. We stood there, united by the simple fact that none of us wanted to get wet. For ten minutes, the city felt like a porch.\n\nWater is patient. It finds the cracks in everything\u2014roads, windows, plans. There is a lesson in that, though I\u2019m not sure what it is. Maybe only that softness can be persistent too. That the things which change us most are often the things we cannot hold back. We can carry umbrellas, wear boots, check the forecast, but eventually we give in and walk through it, letting the rain do what it has always done: touch everything, ask nothing, and move on."
